          },
          "rows": [
            {
              "cards": [
                {
                  "component": {
                    "name": "markdown-card",
                    "settings": {
                      "cardId": "cath8mty9",
                      "schemaVersion": 2.1,
                      "markdown": "<!-- DO NOT MODIFY -->\n\n<div id=\"about-section\"></div>\n\n<h1>How to use this dashboard</h1>\n\n"
                    }
                  },
                  "width": 12
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "cards": [
                {
                  "component": {
                    "name": "markdown-card",
                    "settings": {
                      "cardId": "cioguhye0",
                      "schemaVersion": 2.1,
                      "markdown": "<div class=\"grid-container fluid\">\n    <div class=\"multicolumn-content--columns large-up-3 medium-up-1\"><div class=\"cell\"><div class=\"multicolumn-content\"><h2>View the data -</h2><p>For best results:</p><ul><li>View the dashboard in full screen.</li><li>Use Edge, Chrome, Safari or Firefox as your browser.</li></ul><h2>Read the data -</h2><h3>Header</h3><p>Each page contains text at the top with the dashboard title. Below this section is a table with links to the Minneapolis neighborhood census data dashboards. You can use this dashboard to download the data found on any of them.</p><p>Below the links is information on where to obtain American Community Survey (ACS) data from the U.S. Census Bureau. The only data maintained by the City of Minneapolis for download is the neighborhood level data. Other data must be downloaded from the U.S. Census Bureau.</p><p>You can also view or download a neighborhood boundary spatial file, using the provided link.</p></div></div></div></div>"
                    }
                  },
                  "width": 6
                },
                {
                  "component": {
                    "name": "markdown-card",
                    "settings": {
                      "cardId": "cwc1qrs52",
                      "schemaVersion": 2.1,
                      "markdown": "<div class=\"grid-container fluid\">\n    <div class=\"multicolumn-content--columns large-up-3 medium-up-1\"><div class=\"cell\"><div class=\"multicolumn-content\"><h3>Navigation buttons</h3><p>There are buttons to select which data you would like to download. The first button will allow you to download a data dictionary.&nbsp;</p><p>The other buttons let you choose to download data from one of the dashboards listed at the top of the page. You can also download all neighborhood level ACS data maintained by the City of Minneapolis. This includes neighborhood level data not displayed on the dashboards.</p><p>There is a control to choose which ACS release to download. You can choose to:</p><ul><li>Download the current release displayed on the dashboards</li><li>Download the prior five years of releases</li></ul><p>Please note: The City maintains a rolling six years of neighborhood level ACS data releases. We do not archive neighborhood data older than that. We only provide neighborhood level data using the ACS five year tables. We do not aggregate one year tables to neighborhood.</p><p>You can filter the data before you download. This can help you download only the data you want. It is also helpful if you find the download is too slow or fails to work. You can filter based on the following:</p><ul><li>Dataset ID (found at the top of each page of the neighborhood level dashboards)</li><li>Neighborhood Name</li><li>Reliability Level (see below for information on how reliability level is calculated)</li><li>ACS5 Release (only available if you choose to download the past five releases)</li></ul><p>Once you have made your selections, you can view the data table. To download a data table, look in the upper right corner. There is a button to export to Excel.</p><div><br></div></div></div></div></div>"
                    }
                  },
                  "width": 6
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "cards": [
                {
                  "component": {
                    "name": "markdown-card",
                    "settings": {
                      "cardId": "cjf2o0fq7",
                      "schemaVersion": 2.1,
                      "markdown": "<h3 style=\"font-family:  Avenir Next ,  Avenir Next ; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-size: 28px;\"><span style=\"background-color: initial;\">Technical notes</span></h3>\n<h3 style=\"font-family:  Avenir Next ,  Avenir Next ; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-size: 24px;\">Source of data</h3>\n<h3 style=\"font-family:  Avenir Next ,  Avenir Next ; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-size: 28px;\"><p style=\"font-size: 16px;\">The data in this dashboard come from the U.S. Census Bureau. For more information:</p></h3>\n\n<p style=\"font-size: 16px;\">\n\n•<span style=\"white-space: pre;\"></span>\n<a href=\"https://www.census.gov/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Hyperlink to U.S. Census Bureau website\" style=\"color: rgb(32, 110, 173);\">Visit the U.S. Census Bureau<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' height='20' width='20' viewBox='0 -70 640 640'><path fill='#206EAD' d='M384 64C366.3 64 352 78.3 352 96C352 113.7 366.3 128 384 128L466.7 128L265.3 329.4C252.8 341.9 252.8 362.2 265.3 374.7C277.8 387.2 298.1 387.2 310.6 374.7L512 173.3L512 256C512 273.7 526.3 288 544 288C561.7 288 576 273.7 576 256L576 96C576 78.3 561.7 64 544 64L384 64zM144 160C99.8 160 64 195.8 64 240L64 496C64 540.2 99.8 576 144 576L400 576C444.2 576 480 540.2 480 496L480 416C480 398.3 465.7 384 448 384C430.3 384 416 398.3 416 416L416 496C416 504.8 408.8 512 400 512L144 512C135.2 512 128 504.8 128 496L128 240C128 231.2 135.2 224 144 224L224 224C241.7 224 256 209.7 256 192C256 174.3 241.7 160 224 160L144 160z'></path></svg></a></p>\n\n<p style=\"font-size: 16px;\">\n\n•<span style=\"white-space: pre;\"></span>\n<a href=\"https://data.census.gov/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Hyperlink to Census data website\" style=\"color: rgb(32, 110, 173);\">Find Census data\n<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' height='20' width='20' viewBox='0 -70 640 640'><path fill='#206EAD' d='M384 64C366.3 64 352 78.3 352 96C352 113.7 366.3 128 384 128L466.7 128L265.3 329.4C252.8 341.9 252.8 362.2 265.3 374.7C277.8 387.2 298.1 387.2 310.6 374.7L512 173.3L512 256C512 273.7 526.3 288 544 288C561.7 288 576 273.7 576 256L576 96C576 78.3 561.7 64 544 64L384 64zM144 160C99.8 160 64 195.8 64 240L64 496C64 540.2 99.8 576 144 576L400 576C444.2 576 480 540.2 480 496L480 416C480 398.3 465.7 384 448 384C430.3 384 416 398.3 416 416L416 496C416 504.8 408.8 512 400 512L144 512C135.2 512 128 504.8 128 496L128 240C128 231.2 135.2 224 144 224L224 224C241.7 224 256 209.7 256 192C256 174.3 241.7 160 224 160L144 160z'></path></svg></a></p>\n\n<h3 style=\"font-family:  Avenir Next ,  Avenir Next ; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-size: 24px;\">American Community Survey</h3>\n\n<h3 style=\"font-family:  Avenir Next ,  Avenir Next ; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-size: 28px;\"><p style=\"font-size: 16px;\">The city calculates neighborhood estimates using the American Community Survey (ACS). The U.S. Census Bureau conducts the ACS annually. The five year ACS tables are used for these calculations. Five year tables include survey data from five years.</p><p style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Not all five year ACS tables contain enough information to produce neighborhood data. Therefore, not all ACS tables are included in the city’s dashboards. We do not use one year tables to calculate neighborhood estimates. One year ACS tables do not contain enough information to produce neighborhood data.</p></h3><h3 style=\"font-family:  Avenir Next ,  Avenir Next ; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-size: 24px;\">Margin of error</h3><h3 style=\"font-family:  Avenir Next ,  Avenir Next ; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-size: 28px;\"><p style=\"font-size: 16px;\">ACS data are statistical estimates based on population samples, rather than population counts. These data do not represent true population values, and they include error. A measure of this error is called the “Margin of Error” (MOE).</p>\n<p style=\"font-size: 16px;\">For example, a population estimate may be written as “52 +/- 18”, where “18” is the MOE. The MOE is subtracted from and added to the estimate to create a range. In this case the range is 34 to 70. This range indicates there is a 90% chance the true population count falls between 34 and 70. There is a 10% chance the true value falls outside of the range.</p><p style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Note that if the bottom of the range is a negative number, it is assumed to be zero if a negative number doesn’t make sense. For example, if the estimated number of people under age ten is 124 +/- 200, it doesn’t make sense to say there are -76 children. The range is instead interpreted as 0 to 324.</p></h3><h3 style=\"font-family:  Avenir Next ,  Avenir Next ; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-size: 24px;\">Understanding the estimate reliability</h3><h3 style=\"font-family:  Avenir Next ,  Avenir Next ; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-size: 28px;\"><p style=\"font-size: 16px;\">A statistical value called the Coefficient of Variation (CV) measures how big an MOE is compared to an estimate. An estimate is assigned a reliability level based on the value of the CV.</p>\n<p style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Smaller CVs indicate the MOE is small compared to the estimate. Such estimates are more likely to be close to the true population count. Estimates with larger CVs are less likely to be close to the true population count.</p></h3><h3 style=\"font-family:  Avenir Next ,  Avenir Next ; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-size: 24px;\">Estimate reliability level</h3><h3 style=\"font-family:  Avenir Next ,  Avenir Next ; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-size: 28px;\"><p style=\"font-size: 16px;\">An estimate reliability level is assigned based on the CV as follows:</p><ul><li style=\"font-size: 16px;\">High: CV = 12%</li><li style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Medium: CV between 12% and 40%</li><li style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Low: CV = 40%</li></ul></h3><h3 style=\"font-family:  Avenir Next ,  Avenir Next ; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-size: 24px;\">Interpreting the reliability level</h3><h3 style=\"font-family:  Avenir Next ,  Avenir Next ; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-size: 28px;\">\n<p style=\"font-size: 16px;\">How you interpret the reliability of an estimate depends on how you are using the estimate. There are no specific rules for interpretation. Here is an example of how you might use the reliability level:</p>\n<p style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Suppose there are 51,293 +/- 1,681 children under age ten in Minneapolis. Of these, 349 +/- 399 children under ten live in the Kenwood neighborhood. The estimate reliability is low.</p><ul><li style=\"font-size: 16px;\">You are organizing a children’s festival in Minneapolis, and you want to locate it near a lot of children under ten. You can be confident that Kenwood is not a good neighborhood to pick.</li>\n<li style=\"font-size: 16px;\">You are starting a daycare to serve fifteen children under ten. You want to pick a facility close to where children live. Suppose you find a building in the Kenwood neighborhood. Do not rely on this estimate to decide whether to rent the building. Instead, seek more reliable information about the presence of children under ten in the neighborhood.</li></ul></h3>"
                    }
                  },
                  "width": 12
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "visibility": {
            "access": "default",
            "groups": []
          }
